Upgrade procedure for zlib

Source: https://zlib.net/zlib-VERSION.tar.gz
	(older releases: https://zlib.net/fossils/zlib-VERSION.tar.gz)
	Check zlib-VERSION.tar.gz.asc, signed by Mark Adler,
	5ED46A6721D365587791E2AA783FCD8E58BCAFBA.  The .asc files are also
	attached to the GitHub releases at
	https://github.com/madler/zlib/releases/download/vVERSION/
	(zlib.net/fossils has no .asc files).

The vendor branch (vendor/zlib) carries the complete release tarball.
Pruning happens only in sys/contrib/zlib; see MIDNIGHTBSD-Xlist.  The
tarballs since 1.3.2 contain two empty directories under test/, which
git cannot store.

sys/contrib/zlib is used by lib/libz, by the kernel (sys/conf/files,
sys/modules/zlib) and by the boot loader (stand/libsa and the
-I${SRCTOP}/sys/contrib/zlib users in stand/).

To upgrade to a new version:

1. Download and verify the tarball and extract it into a new directory.
2. Use the vendor-update skill (vendor/zlib, sys/contrib/zlib, tag
   vendor/zlib/VERSION).  Stage with "git add -A -f".
3. Merge.  Files we prune but upstream changed show up as modify/delete
   conflicts, and new upstream files (CMake, Bazel and so on) are added
   by the merge; remove everything MIDNIGHTBSD-Xlist matches and extend
   the Xlist for new files we do not want.
4. Re-check the local patches listed below, especially zconf.h.
5. lib/libz: add new exported functions to Symbol.map under a new
   ZLIB_<version> node (compare zlib.map and the ZEXTERN declarations
   in zlib.h) and add the node to Versions.def; update the version in
   zlib.pc.  SHLIB_MAJOR stays 6 as long as no symbol is removed.
   FreeBSD's lib/libz is a good reference.
6. Build lib/libz, the kernel (or sys/modules/zlib) and stand/, and run
   upstream's test/example.c and test/minigzip.c against the new libz.

Branch policy: stable/4.0 stays on zlib 1.3.x to keep the change for
ports small; security fixes from newer releases are backported there
as separate commits.  master and stable/4.1 follow upstream releases.

Local patches applied on top of upstream (from FreeBSD unless noted):

  - zconf.h, top of file: under __FreeBSD__ (which our compilers also
    define), define Z_SOLO for _KERNEL, and for Z_SOLO include
    <sys/types.h> and define Z_U8/Z_U4 as __uint64_t/__uint32_t.
  - zconf.h, Z_PREFIX list: the compress*/uncompress* and
    zcalloc/zcfree prefix blocks test
    "!defined(Z_SOLO) && !defined(_KERNEL)" instead of
    "#ifndef Z_SOLO".
  - zconf.h: "#if HAVE_UNISTD_H-0" is replaced by
    "#if 0    /* was set to #if 0 by ./configure */", so <zlib.h>
    never defines Z_HAVE_UNISTD_H and does not include <unistd.h> in
    consumers.  FreeBSD's 1.3.2 import instead defines HAVE_UNISTD_H
    and HAVE_STDARG_H to 1 (0 and a NULL definition in the kernel) at
    the top of zconf.h; MidnightBSD keeps the old behaviour so that
    consumers see the same preprocessor environment as with 1.3.1.
  - zconf.h (MidnightBSD): "#if HAVE_STDARG_H-0" is replaced by
    1.3.1's "#ifdef HAVE_STDARG_H".  Upstream's form makes <zlib.h>
    fail with -Wundef -Werror (bxe(4) and any such port) unless
    HAVE_STDARG_H is defined, and FreeBSD's way around it (defining
    HAVE_UNISTD_H and HAVE_STDARG_H in zconf.h) would leak those
    autoconf-style macros into every consumer.
  - zconf.h, after the SEEK_SET block: "This is hard-configured for
    FreeBSD": include <sys/types.h> for Z_SOLO, define z_off_t as
    off_t, and default _FILE_OFFSET_BITS to 64.  This makes z_off_t
    64 bits on every architecture and keeps the gz*64 names out of the
    ABI, whatever upstream's default for z_off_t is (long long in
    1.3.2, long again in 1.4).
  - zlib.h: the utility functions (compress, uncompress, ...) are
    declared for "!defined(Z_SOLO) || defined(_KERNEL)" and the gzip
    file functions keep "#ifndef Z_SOLO" (the #endif/#ifndef pair is
    inserted before the "gzip file access functions" comment).
  - zlib.h: a crc32_combine_gen64() prototype before the "various
    hacks, don't look :)" section.
  - zutil.h: the zcalloc()/zcfree() prototypes are declared for
    "!defined(Z_SOLO) || defined(_KERNEL)".
  - deflate.c, infback.c, inflate.c: in deflateInit2_(),
    inflateBackInit_() and inflateInit2_() the default zalloc/zfree
    are used for "!(defined(Z_SOLO) && !defined(_KERNEL))", so the
    kernel gets zcalloc/zcfree from sys/dev/zlib/zcalloc.c.
  - deflate.c: "#if !defined(MIN)" around the MIN() macro before
    deflate_stored().
  - deflate.h: LIT_MEM is defined.
  - gzlib.c: #include "zutil.h" and <unistd.h> after "gzguts.h";
    gzread.c, gzwrite.c: #include <unistd.h> after "gzguts.h".
  - contrib/gcc_gvmat64/gvmat64.S: kept as a copy of the 1.3.1 file
    with CRs stripped.  It is not built.  Upstream converted it to LF
    and changed it in 1.3.2, so the merge conflicts; the file is
    assembly and was left untouched.  FreeBSD dropped contrib/
    entirely in its 1.3.2 import.
